{"name":"Coastal Crush","hex":"#b4c0af","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coastal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to the coast; on or near the coast; longshore."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coastal"},{"term":"crush","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A violent collision or compression; a crash; destruction; ruin.","Violent pressure, as of a moving crowd."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To press between two hard objects; to squeeze so as to alter the natural shape or integrity, or to force together into a mass.","To reduce to fine particles by pounding or grinding."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coast","blocks":[{"text":"A coast is the land next to the sea or the line that forms the boundary between the land and the ocean or a lake. Coasts are influenced by the topography of the surrounding landscape and by aquatic erosion, such as that caused by waves. The geological composition of rock and soil dictates the type of shore that is created. Earth has about 620,000.0 km (385,250.1 mi) of coastline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg/330px-Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast","wikidata":"Q93352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Knights_Point_-_Tasm%C3%A1nsk%C3%A9_mo%C5%99e_-_panoramio.jpg/500px-Knights_Point_-_Tasm%C3%A1nsk%C3%A9_mo%C5%99e_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Knights_Point_-_Tasm%C3%A1nsk%C3%A9_mo%C5%99e_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Knights_Point_-_Tasm%C3%A1nsk%C3%A9_mo%C5%99e_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src